| editorial =
'''The fugitive — justice must reach beyond borders'''

Ronaldo Batot walked out of Okaiken on 12 July. Three days after the Akamatsu Group arrests. Two days before investigators arrived at his Okami apartment with a search warrant. He flew to Castejón, Balisca, then to Maros, Pangkajene — a country with no extradition treaty.

The question is not whether Batot fled. He did. The question is how. How did a central figure in two of the largest criminal investigations in Okaiken's recent history leave the country while under active investigation? Who knew? Who acted? Who failed to act?

The Justice Ministry issued a worldwide arrest warrant on 16 July. Four days after Batot's flight. The Interpol Red Notice was requested the same day. Four days. In those four days, Batot crossed two international borders, entered a country with no extradition obligation, and disappeared into a city of 1.4 million people.

Border exit records show Batot used his Pangkajenen passport — not his Okaiken residency card. He was not on a watch list. He was not flagged. The Akamatsu Group arrests on 9 July named three executives. Batot was not among them. He was a consultant, not an employee. The distinction may have been enough.

The Mainichi has supported the IAA Commission, the prosecution, and the investigative framework. We continue to support them. But support is not deference. The system that allowed Batot to flee is the same system that allowed the Akamatsu Group to embezzle ¥18B from employee pension funds for three years without detection. The same system that allowed uninspected waste to leave Okaiken for Balisca. The same system that allowed an 11-day silence from the Foreign Ministry.

Pangkajene President Pattawari says he is "reviewing." He will review slowly. He has no treaty obligation. He has domestic politics. Pangkajene has 61 million people, a maritime economy, and no incentive to antagonise Okaiken — but no incentive to cooperate quickly either. The diplomatic work will take weeks. The legal work will take months. Batot has time.

The Justice Ministry must answer for the gap. The prosecution must explain why Batot was not arrested on 9 July alongside the three executives. The Foreign Ministry must explain why border controls were not tightened after the arrests. The IAA Commission must examine whether the investigative framework — designed for institutional accountability — has the operational reach to prevent flight.

Justice that cannot cross borders is not justice. It is procedure. Okaiken has the procedure. It does not yet have Batot.

